S="Advent: O Come, Emmanuel"
D("2026-12-14",S,"A Shoot from a Stump","a 3rd–5th grader","Isaiah 11:1–2",[(1,"There shall come forth a shoot from the stump of Jesse, and a branch from his roots shall bear fruit."),(2,"And the Spirit of the LORD shall rest upon him, the Spirit of wisdom and understanding, the Spirit of counsel and might, the Spirit of knowledge and the fear of the LORD.")],
"The Jesse tree",[
"Remember Jesse, David’s father? From Jesse’s family came King David and many kings after him. But over the years, the kings turned away from God, and finally enemies conquered Jerusalem. David’s royal family was like a great tree that had been chopped down. Only a stump was left.",
"But Isaiah gave a promise: from that dead-looking stump, a little green shoot would grow! A new King would come from Jesse’s family, and God’s Spirit would rest on Him. He would be wise and fair. He would care for the poor. And under His rule, the wolf would lie down with the lamb, and a little child would lead them.",
"For hundreds of years, it looked like the stump was dead. But God hadn’t forgotten. Jesus was born from the family of Jesse and David. He is the shoot from the stump. Many families make a “Jesse tree” in Advent, hanging an ornament for each Bible story that leads up to Jesus, from creation to Christmas."],
"New life from a stump",["When things look dead and hopeless, God can make something new grow. That’s what He did through Jesus, and it’s what He does in our lives too. The whole Old Testament is like a family tree growing toward Jesus."],
["What was left after the tree was chopped down? What grew out of it?","What will it be like when the new King rules, according to Isaiah?","Look back at all the stories we’ve read since October. How do they make a “Jesse tree” leading to Jesus?"],
"Lord God, You made new life grow from the stump of Jesse. Thank You for Jesus, the King who rules with wisdom and love.","Where things feel dead or hopeless, make new life grow.",
"From Jesse’s chopped-down stump, a little shoot grew. Jesus is that shoot, the new King!",
["Jesse was King [David]’s father.","The tree was cut down to a [stump].","A little green [shoot] grew.","God’s [Spirit] would rest on Him.","The shoot is [Jesus]."],
["JESSE","STUMP","SHOOT","BRANCH","SPIRIT","ROOTS"],[("Was the stump really dead?",["Yes","No"],1),("Who is the shoot from Jesse?",["Jesus","Samuel"],0)],
"Draw a stump with a green shoot growing from it.",("Isaiah 11:1","“There shall come forth a shoot from the stump of Jesse.”"),
scene(hills(),'<path d="M270,300 V230 Q320,214 370,230 V300 Z"/><ellipse cx="320" cy="230" rx="50" ry="12"/><path d="M300,230 q4,6 0,10 M330,232 q6,4 2,10" fill="none" stroke-width="1"/>','<path d="M320,226 Q314,180 328,140" fill="none" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M322,190 q-24,-14 -30,-34 q24,4 30,34 Z M326,160 q20,-18 34,-22 q-6,22 -34,22 Z M328,140 q-10,-20 0,-34 q10,14 0,34 Z"/>',sheep(140),'<path d="M480,280 q-10,-30 20,-36 l20,-18 l8,14 l20,0 q20,10 10,40 Z"/><circle cx="526" cy="236" r="1.6" class="dot"/>',label="Coloring picture: a new shoot growing from a stump, with a lamb and a wolf"))

D("2026-12-15",S,"Jesus’ Family Tree","an older kid","Matthew 1:1",[(1,"The book of the genealogy of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham.")],
"The genealogy",[
"The very first page of the New Testament might look boring: a long list of names. “Abraham was the father of Isaac, and Isaac the father of Jacob, and Jacob the father of Judah…” But it’s actually one of the most amazing pages in the Bible. It’s Jesus’ family tree.",
"Look closely and you’ll find people we’ve met: Abraham who counted the stars, Isaac on the mountain, Jacob with his ladder, Judah who offered his life for Benjamin, Ruth who said “Your people will be my people,” and David the shepherd king. God kept His promises through every generation.",
"But you’ll also find surprises. There’s Rahab, a woman from Jericho who wasn’t an Israelite. There are kings who did terrible things. Jesus’ family tree is full of sinners and outsiders. Jesus didn’t come from a perfect family. He came into a real, messy family, because He came to save real, messy people."],
"Grace in the family tree",["Matthew includes the scandals on purpose. Jesus isn’t ashamed to call sinners His family. Hebrews 2:11 says He is not ashamed to call us brothers and sisters."],
["Name some people from Jesus’ family tree we’ve read about.","Why would Matthew start his Gospel with a family tree?","Jesus’ family tree includes sinners and outsiders. What does that tell you about the kind of people Jesus came for?"],
"Lord Jesus, thank You for coming into a real family full of real sinners, and for not being ashamed to call us Your family.","Thank You for our family, and for all the generations who passed down the faith to us.",
"Jesus’ family tree includes Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Ruth, and David. God kept His promise through every generation!",
["Jesus’ family tree is called a [genealogy].","It starts with [Abraham].","It includes Ruth and King [David].","It even includes [Rahab] from Jericho.","Jesus is not [ashamed] to call us family."],
["ABRAHAM","DAVID","RUTH","JACOB","FAMILY","TREE"],[("Was Jesus’ family perfect?",["Yes","No"],1),("Did God keep His promise through every generation?",["Yes","No"],0)],
"Draw your own family tree with names on the branches.",("Matthew 1:1","“The book of the genealogy of Jesus Christ.”"),
scene(tree(320,260),''.join(f'<circle cx="{x}" cy="{y}" r="9"/>' for x,y in [(270,90),(320,70),(370,90),(250,130),(390,130),(300,120),(340,120)]),scroll(130,250),manger(520),label="Coloring picture: a family tree leading to the manger"))

D("2026-12-16",S,"Comfort, Comfort My People","a K–2nd grader, with help","Isaiah 40:1, 11",[(1,"Comfort, comfort my people, says your God."),(11,"He will tend his flock like a shepherd; he will gather the lambs in his arms; he will carry them in his bosom, and gently lead those that are with young.")],
"God’s gentle promise",[
"God’s people had been through a very hard time. Enemies had taken them far from home, to Babylon. Daniel and his friends were part of that time. The people felt forgotten. They thought, “God must be done with us.”",
"But God told Isaiah to bring a new message, a gentle one: “Comfort, comfort My people. Speak tenderly to them. Tell them their hard time is over and their sins are forgiven.” God wasn’t done with them at all!",
"Isaiah described God like a shepherd who picks up the little lambs that can’t keep up, carries them close to His heart, and walks slowly with the mother sheep. God isn’t rough or in a hurry. He is gentle with the weak and the little ones."],
"Tenderness",["Some people think of God as angry or far away. But Isaiah shows us a God who scoops up little lambs and carries them close. That’s exactly who Jesus is. He took children in His arms and blessed them."],
["What does God tell Isaiah to say? What does the shepherd do with the little lambs?","Why did God’s people feel forgotten?","How does Isaiah 40 change the way you picture God? When do you need God to carry you?"],
"Gentle Shepherd, thank You for comforting Your people and carrying the little lambs. Thank You for forgiving our sins.","When we are tired or sad, carry us close to Your heart.",
"God said, “Comfort, comfort My people!” He carries the little lambs close to His heart.",
["God said, “[Comfort], comfort My people.”","God’s people felt [forgotten].","He will tend His [flock] like a shepherd.","He carries the [lambs] in His arms.","God is [gentle] with little ones."],
["COMFORT","LAMBS","GENTLE","FLOCK","CARRY","TENDER"],[("Is God rough with little lambs?",["Yes","No"],1),("Where does the shepherd carry the lambs?",["Close to his heart","Far away"],0)],
"Draw the shepherd carrying a lamb in his arms.",("Isaiah 40:1","“Comfort, comfort my people, says your God.”"),
scene(hills(),person(260,beard=True,arms="open",hair="short"),'<g transform="translate(262,206) scale(.5)"><path d="M-30,-14 q-6,-14 8,-18 q4,-14 18,-10 q10,-12 22,-2 q14,-4 16,10 q12,6 4,20 q4,12 -12,14 h-44 q-16,-2 -12,-14 Z"/><ellipse cx="38" cy="-22" rx="11" ry="8"/></g>',sheep(420),sheep(510,.75),lamb(580),sun(80,60),label="Coloring picture: a shepherd carrying a little lamb"))

D("2026-12-17",S,"God with Us","an older kid","Isaiah 7:14",[(14,"Therefore the Lord himself will give you a sign. Behold, the virgin shall conceive and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel.")],
"O Come, O Come, Emmanuel",[
"For more than a thousand years, starting on December 17, Christians have sung seven special prayers called the “O Antiphons,” one each night until Christmas Eve. Each one calls Jesus by an Old Testament name: O Wisdom, O Lord, O Root of Jesse, O Key of David, O Dayspring, O King of the Nations, and O Emmanuel.",
"Later, those prayers were made into a hymn you probably know: “O Come, O Come, Emmanuel.” Its verses pray, “O come, Thou Rod of Jesse’s stem… O come, Thou Dayspring from on high… O come, Thou Key of David, come.” It’s a song of waiting, sung by people longing for the Savior.",
"The last name, Emmanuel (or Immanuel), comes from tonight’s verse. Seven hundred years before Jesus, Isaiah said a virgin would have a son and name Him Immanuel, which means “God with us.” And Matthew tells us that this came true when Mary had baby Jesus. God didn’t stay far away. He came to be with us."],
"Emmanuel",["“God with us” is the heart of Christmas. God didn’t send a message or an angel to fix things. He came Himself. And Jesus’ last promise in Matthew is, “I am with you always, to the end of the age.”"],
["What does Emmanuel mean? What hymn uses the O Antiphons?","Why is it good news that God came to be with us instead of staying far away?","Jesus’ name Emmanuel appears at the start of Matthew, and His promise “I am with you always” at the end. Why do you think Matthew bookends his Gospel that way?"],
"O come, O come, Emmanuel! Thank You, Lord Jesus, for coming to be God with us.","Be with us always, in every day and every night.",
"Isaiah said the baby would be called Emmanuel, which means “God with us”!",
["Emmanuel means “God [with] us.”","The prophet was [Isaiah].","The baby would be born to a [virgin].","We sing “O Come, O Come, [Emmanuel].”","Jesus said, “I am with you [always].”"],
["EMMANUEL","WITH","SIGN","VIRGIN","ALWAYS","COME"],[("Does Emmanuel mean “God with us”?",["Yes","No"],0),("Did God stay far away from us?",["Yes","No"],1)],
"Write “EMMANUEL” in big letters and decorate it.",("Isaiah 7:14","“And shall call his name Immanuel.”"),
scene(bigstar(320,50),wreath(320,3),candles(60,2,2),candles(500,2,2),label="Coloring picture: an Advent wreath under the Christmas star"))

D("2026-12-18",S,"A Great Light","a 3rd–5th grader","Isaiah 9:2",[(2,"The people who walked in darkness have seen a great light; those who dwelt in a land of deep darkness, on them has light shone.")],
"Light in the darkness",[
"These are the darkest days of the whole year. The sun sets early and rises late. On Monday it will be the longest night of the year. Long ago, before electricity, the dark of winter could feel scary and long.",
"Isaiah used that picture to describe the world without God: people walking in darkness, unable to see where they’re going, afraid and lost. Sin is like darkness. It makes us stumble, and it hides us from God and from each other.",
"But Isaiah promised a great light would shine. And when Jesus grew up, He said, “I am the light of the world. Whoever follows Me will not walk in darkness, but will have the light of life.” That’s why we light candles at Christmas. Jesus is the light that came into the darkest night."],
"The light of the world",["Every Advent candle we light makes the room a little brighter, until Christmas when the Christ candle is lit. Jesus is the light no darkness can put out (John 1:5)."],
["What is the longest night of the year? Who is the light of the world?","How is sin like darkness?","John 1:5 says the darkness has not overcome the light. What dark things in the world does Jesus’ light shine into?"],
"Lord Jesus, You are the light of the world. Thank You for shining into our darkness.","Help us walk in Your light and shine it for others.",
"Isaiah promised, “The people who walked in darkness have seen a great light!” Jesus is the light of the world!",
["People walked in [darkness].","They saw a great [light].","Jesus is the light of the [world].","Monday is the longest [night].","We light [candles] at Christmas."],
["LIGHT","DARKNESS","CANDLE","SHINE","WORLD","NIGHT"],[("Who is the light of the world?",["Jesus","The sun"],0),("Can darkness overcome Jesus’ light?",["Yes","No"],1)],
"Draw a candle shining in a dark room.",("Isaiah 9:2","“The people who walked in darkness have seen a great light.”"),
scene(stars(24,61,(200,440)),moon(560,56),'<rect x="296" y="160" width="48" height="130"/><path d="M320,160 q-18,-26 0,-56 q18,30 0,56 Z"/>'+''.join(f'<path d="M{320+60*math.cos(a):.0f},{130+60*math.sin(a):.0f} L{320+80*math.cos(a):.0f},{130+80*math.sin(a):.0f}" fill="none" stroke-width="1.4"/>' for a in [math.pi*(1+k/8) for k in range(1,8)]),'<path d="M270,300 h100 v-10 h-100 Z"/>',person(140,beard=False,arms="up",hair="curly",s=.7),person(500,beard=True,arms="up",hair="short",s=.7),label="Coloring picture: a candle shining in the dark night"))

D("2026-12-19",S,"The Road to Bethlehem","a K–2nd grader, with help","Luke 2:1, 4–5",[(1,"In those days a decree went out from Caesar Augustus that all the world should be registered."),(4,"And Joseph also went up from Galilee, from the town of Nazareth, to Judea, to the city of David, which is called Bethlehem, because he was of the house and lineage of David,"),(5,"to be registered with Mary, his betrothed, who was with child.")],
"The long trip",[
"Far away in Rome, the most powerful man in the world, Caesar Augustus, decided to count all the people in his empire, probably so he could collect more taxes. He gave an order: everyone had to go to their family’s hometown to be counted.",
"Joseph’s family came from King David, so he had to go to Bethlehem, David’s town. That was about 90 miles from Nazareth, mostly walking, up and down hills. And Mary was going to have her baby very soon! The trip probably took about a week.",
"Caesar thought he was in charge. But God was using Caesar’s order to bring Mary and Joseph to exactly the right place. Remember Micah’s promise? The King would be born in Bethlehem. God can use even the decisions of emperors to keep His promises."],
"God is in charge",["Mary and Joseph didn’t choose this trip, and it was hard. But God was guiding every step. When our plans get interrupted, God is still working out His plans."],
["Who made everyone go to their hometown? Which town did Mary and Joseph go to?","Why was the trip so hard for Mary?","Caesar thought he was in charge. How did God use Caesar’s order to keep Micah’s promise?"],
"Lord God, You guided Mary and Joseph every step of the way to Bethlehem. Thank You that You are in charge of everything.","When our plans change, help us trust that You are guiding us.",
"Mary and Joseph traveled to Bethlehem, just as God promised. God was guiding every step!",
["The emperor was Caesar [Augustus].","Everyone had to go to be [counted].","Mary and Joseph went to [Bethlehem].","Bethlehem is the city of [David].","Mary was going to have a [baby]."],
["CAESAR","JOURNEY","BETHLEHEM","JOSEPH","MARY","ROAD"],[("Who was really in charge?",["God","Caesar"],0),("Was the trip easy?",["Yes","No"],1)],
"Draw Mary and Joseph on the road to Bethlehem.",("Luke 2:4","“To the city of David, which is called Bethlehem.”"),
scene(hills(),'<path d="M4,300 Q200,250 400,270 T636,240" fill="none" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>',person(170,beard=True,arms="mixed",hair="short"),donkey(330),person(330,beard=False,arms="open",hair="wrap",s=.65),house(560),label="Coloring picture: Mary and Joseph traveling to Bethlehem"))

D("2026-12-20",S,"Twenty-Four Candles","Mom or Dad","Philippians 4:4–5",[(4,"Rejoice in the Lord always; again I will say, rejoice."),(5,"Let your reasonableness be known to everyone. The Lord is at hand;")],
"The first Advent wreath",[
"Today is the fourth and last Sunday of Advent. Tonight all four candles on the Advent wreath are lit. But where did the Advent wreath come from? About 185 years ago, in Hamburg, Germany, there was a Lutheran pastor named Johann Wichern who ran a home for poor and orphaned boys.",
"Every December, the boys kept asking, “Is it Christmas yet? How many more days?” So in 1839, Pastor Wichern took an old wagon wheel and put candles on it: little red ones for each weekday and big white ones for each Sunday of Advent. Every day at prayer time, the boys lit one more candle, and the room got brighter and brighter as Christmas came closer.",
"Other families loved the idea. Over time, the wheel became a circle of evergreen branches, and the candles became just four, one for each Sunday. Now churches and families all over the world light Advent wreaths. It all started with a Lutheran pastor who loved some kids and wanted to help them wait for Jesus."],
"Rejoice, the Lord is near",["Tonight’s verse says, “Rejoice in the Lord always… The Lord is at hand.” The waiting is almost over! The circle of the wreath has no end, like God’s love, and the growing light reminds us that Jesus, the light of the world, is coming."],
["What did Pastor Wichern use for the first Advent wreath? How many candles did it have?","Why did he make it? How did the room change each day?","Why is an evergreen circle a good picture of God’s love and of Jesus?"],
"Lord Jesus, we rejoice, because You are near! Thank You for Pastor Wichern, who helped children wait for You.","Help us wait with joy these last few days before Christmas.",
"A Lutheran pastor made the first Advent wreath from a wagon wheel with candles! Rejoice, the Lord is near!",
["The first Advent wreath was made from a wagon [wheel].","Pastor Wichern helped poor [boys].","He lived in [Germany].","Each day they lit one more [candle].","Rejoice in the [Lord] always!"],
["WREATH","CANDLES","WHEEL","REJOICE","WICHERN","ADVENT"],[("Who made the first Advent wreath?",["A Lutheran pastor","A king"],0),("How many candles are lit tonight?",["Four","One"],0)],
"Draw an Advent wreath with all four candles lit.",("Philippians 4:4","“Rejoice in the Lord always; again I will say, rejoice.”"),
scene(window(470),'<circle cx="170" cy="190" r="90"/><circle cx="170" cy="190" r="14"/>'+''.join(f'<path d="M{170+14*math.cos(a):.0f},{190+14*math.sin(a):.0f} L{170+90*math.cos(a):.0f},{190+90*math.sin(a):.0f}" fill="none"/>' for a in [math.pi*k/6 for k in range(12)])+''.join(f'<rect x="{170+90*math.cos(a)-4:.0f}" y="{190+90*math.sin(a)-22:.0f}" width="8" height="18"/><path d="M{170+90*math.cos(a):.0f},{190+90*math.sin(a)-22:.0f} q-5,-8 0,-14 q5,6 0,14 Z"/>' for a in [math.pi*(1.05+k*0.12) for k in range(8)]),wreath(420,4),label="Coloring picture: Pastor Wichern’s wagon-wheel Advent wreath and a modern wreath"))
